WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.580
Direct prompt injection happens when the user-facing prompt itself attempts
to steer the model away from the intended task.

2
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.580
The application receives the instruction directly from the person using the
interface.

3
00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:20.340
That is serious, but the trust boundary is easier to see: the user input is
untrusted and should not be allowed to override the system’s role, policy,
or authorization model.

4
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:25.540
Indirect prompt injection is different. The attacker does not need direct
access to the prompt box.

5
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:37.460
Instead, instructions are embedded in external content that the model later
processes: a web page, document, email, ticket, repository file, search
result, or tool output.

6
00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:50.180
NIST describes this class as adversaries exploiting LLM-integrated
applications by injecting prompts into data likely to be retrieved. OWASP
similarly treats external content as a prompt injection source.

7
00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:57.700
Jailbreaking overlaps with prompt injection but is not the same category. A
jailbreak tries to defeat model behavior or safety constraints.

8
00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:06.020
Prompt injection is broader: it manipulates the application workflow by
mixing instructions and data. RAG poisoning targets retrieval content or
indexes.

9
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:15.140
Tool-output injection targets data returned from an external tool. Wormable
propagation appears when those influences can move onward to another
AI-enabled surface.

10
00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:17.140
That distinction matters for defense.

11
00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:24.580
If an organization treats prompt injection as only a bad chat message, it
will miss the systems where no user typed the malicious content.

12
00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:32.500
A document processor may retrieve untrusted text. An agent may parse a web
page. A support assistant may summarize inbound email.

13
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:36.260
A workflow bot may read comments and write follow-up tasks.

14
00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:45.140
The practical question is always: where can untrusted content enter, what
can the model see, what can it influence, and what downstream systems may
consume its output?

15
00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:49.620
Wormable prompt injection is the risk that those answers form a loop or
chain.
